We define $f(x) = x\sin(\pi/x)$ for $0 < x \leq 1.$ To prove that the arc length of $f(x)$ from $x=0$ to $x=1/2$ is finite, we examine the arc length $L$ of the function $g(x)=x\sin(\pi/x)$ on the interval $[0,1/2].$ We divide the interval into $n$ equally spaced subintervals, where the width of each subinterval is $\frac{1/2}{n}=\frac{1}{2n}.$ Then the length of the arc is \begin{align*}
L=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sqrt{\left(\frac{d}{dx}g\left(\frac{i}{2n}\right)\right)^2+1}\\
=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sqrt{\left(2\cos^2(\pi i/2n)-1\right)^2+1}\\
=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sqrt{1+4\cos^4(\pi i/2n)-4\cos^2(\pi i/2n)}
\end{align*}Since $0\leq \cos^2 (\pi i/n)\leq 1$ we see that $1\leq L\leq \sqrt{5} n.$ Since $n$ can be made as large as we wish, we see that the arc length $L$ is finite.

To prove that the arc length of $f(x)$ from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is finite, define $D(s)$ to be the sum of the arc lengths of $g(x) = x \sin (x \pi / \sqrt{n})$ for all $n \geq s.$ The inequality \begin{align*}
4 \cos^2 (\pi \sqrt{2r+n}) \leq 1
\end{align*}that we derived earlier holds with equality if and only if $r$ and $n$ are simultaneously squares of integers. Thus the sum $D(s)$ includes an additional term of $L(s) = \sqrt{1+3\cos^2 (\pi/2s)}$ for each square beyond $1$, so we have $D(s + n^2) \geq D(s) + \sqrt{1+3\cos^2 (\pi/2s)}.$ In particular $D(s) \geq  \sum_{\sqrt{t}<s}\sqrt{1+3\cos^2 (\pi/2s)}$ where the summation denotes over all noteworthy integers less than $n$. This final sum clearly has no bound as $s$ increases.

